    {
      "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-international-crime-cooperation-and-other-measures-act-2018-sch-1-sec-32a",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "32A Authorising applications for surveillance device warrants",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-legislation-amendment-international-crime-cooperation-and-other-measures-act-2018#sch-1-sec-32a"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "crimes-legislation-amendment-international-crime-cooperation-and-other-measures-act-2018.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "crimes-legislation-amendment-international-crime-cooperation-and-other-measures-act-2018.pdf, p.38",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Attorney-General may authorise, in writing, an eligible law\nenforcement officer to apply for a surveillance device warrant\nunder section 14 of the Surveillance Devices Act 2004 if:\n\n(a) a Tribunal has requested the Attorney-General to arrange for\nthe use of a surveillance device; and\n\n(b) the Attorney-General is satisfied that a proceeding is before,\nor an investigation is being conducted by, the Tribunal; and\n\n(c) the Attorney-General is satisfied that the Tribunal has given\nappropriate undertakings for:\n\n(i) ensuring that the information obtained as a result of the\nuse of the device will only be used for the purpose for\nwhich it is communicated to the Tribunal; and\n\n(ii) the destruction of a document or other thing containing\ninformation obtained as a result of the use of the device;\nand\n\n(iii) any other matter the Attorney-General considers\nappropriate.\n\nAssistance to international courts and tribunals  Schedule 1\nUse of surveillance devices  Part 6\n\nNo. 34, 2018\nCrimes Legislation Amendment (International Crime Cooperation and\nOther Measures) Act 2018\n\nNote:\nThe eligible law enforcement officer can only apply for the warrant if\nhe or she reasonably suspects that the use of the device is necessary\nfor the investigation or proceeding (see subsection 14(3A) of the\nSurveillance Devices Act 2004).\n\n(2) An eligible law enforcement officer is a person mentioned in\ncolumn 3 of table item 5 in subsection 6A(6), or column 3 of table\nitem 5 in subsection 6A(7), of the Surveillance Devices Act 2004.\nSurveillance Devices Act 2004",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The Attorney-General may authorise, in writing, an eligible law\nenforcement officer to apply for a surveillance device warrant\nunder section 14 of the Surveillance Devices Act 2004 if:\n\n(a) a Tribunal has requested the Attorney-General to arrange for\nthe use of a surveillance device; and\n\n(b) the Attorney-General is satisfied that a proceeding is before,\nor an investigation is being conducted by, the Tribunal; and\n\n(c) the Attorney-General is satisfied that the Tribunal has given\nappropriate undertakings for:\n\n(i) ensuring that the information obtained as a result of the\nuse of the device will only be used for the purpose for\nwhich it is communicated to the Tribunal; and\n\n(ii) the destruction of a document or other thing containing\ninformation obtained as a result of the use of the device;\nand\n\n(iii) any other matter the Attorney-General considers\nappropriate.\n\nAssistance to international courts and tribunals  Schedule 1\nUse of surveillance devices  Part 6\n\nNo. 34, 2018\nCrimes Legislation Amendment (International Crime Cooperation and\nOther Measures) Act 2018\n\nNote:\nThe eligible law enforcement officer can only apply for the warrant if\nhe or she reasonably suspects that the use of the device is necessary\nfor the investigation or proceeding (see subsection 14(3A) of the\nSurveillance Devices Act 2004).\n\n(2) An eligible law enforcement officer is a person mentioned in\ncolumn 3 of table item 5 in subsection 6A(6), or column 3 of table\nitem 5 in subsection 6A(7), of the Surveillance Devices Act 2004.\nSurveillance Devices Act 2004",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-international-crime-cooperation-and-other-measures-act-2018-sch-1-sec-32a-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:crimes-legislation-amendment-international-crime-cooperation-and-other-measures-act-2018-sch-1-sec-32a-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "74a9bb2c4544ea5ff9c8462c27c21022f7761aee58cd46dd914dcc7b8446a087"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/crimes-legislation-amendment-international-crime-cooperation-and-other-measures-act-2018#sch-1-sec-32a",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "32A Authorising applications for surveillance device warrants",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 38,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Attorney-General may authorise, in writing, an eligible law\nenforcement officer to apply for a surveillance device warrant\nunder section 14 of the Surveillance Devices Act 2004 if:\n\n(a) a Tribunal has requested the Attorney-General to arrange for\nthe use of a surveillance device; and\n\n(b) the Attorney-General is satisfied that a proceeding is before,\nor an investigation is being conducted by, the Tribunal; and\n\n(c) the Attorney-General is satisfied that the Tribunal has given\nappropriate undertakings for:\n\n(i) ensuring that the information obtained as a result of the\nuse of the device will only be used for the purpose for\nwhich it is communicated to the Tribunal; and\n\n(ii) the destruction of a document or other thing containing\ninformation obtained as a result of the use of the device;\nand\n\n(iii) any other matter the Attorney-General considers\nappropriate.\n\nAssistance to international courts and tribunals  Schedule 1\nUse of surveillance devices  Part 6\n\nNo. 34, 2018\nCrimes Legislation Amendment (International Crime Cooperation and\nOther Measures) Act 2018\n\nNote:\nThe eligible law enforcement officer can only apply for the warrant if\nhe or she reasonably suspects that the use of the device is necessary\nfor the investigation or proceeding (see subsection 14(3A) of the\nSurveillance Devices Act 2004).\n\n(2) An eligible law enforcement officer is a person mentioned in\ncolumn 3 of table item 5 in subsection 6A(6), or column 3 of table\nitem 5 in subsection 6A(7), of the Surveillance Devices Act 2004.\nSurveillance Devices Act 2004"
    },
